## Solution

We evaluate
$$L = \lim_{n\to\infty}\int_{[0,\pi/4]} \sin(nx)\,\log(1+x^n)\,d\lambda(x).$$

**Key observation.** Since $\pi/4 \approx 0.7854 < 1$, every point $x\in[0,\pi/4]$ satisfies $0\le x\le \pi/4 < 1$. Hence for every such $x$,
$$x^n \xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{} 0,$$
and therefore, by continuity of $\log(1+\cdot)$ at $0$,
$$\log(1+x^n) \xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{} \log(1+0)=0.$$

**Pointwise limit of the integrand.** Since $|\sin(nx)|\le 1$ for all $n$ and $x$, we get
$$\bigl|\sin(nx)\log(1+x^n)\bigr|\le \log(1+x^n)\to 0,$$
so the integrand $f_n(x):=\sin(nx)\log(1+x^n)$ converges pointwise to $0$ on $[0,\pi/4]$.

**Dominating function (independent of $n$).** Using $\log(1+t)\le t$ for $t\ge 0$ and $x\le \pi/4<1$:
$$|f_n(x)|\le \log(1+x^n)\le x^n\le (\pi/4)^n\le \pi/4 \qquad(n\ge 1,\;x\in[0,\pi/4]).$$
The constant $g(x)\equiv \pi/4$ is integrable on the finite interval $[0,\pi/4]$:
$$\int_{[0,\pi/4]} g\,d\lambda = \tfrac{\pi}{4}\cdot\tfrac{\pi}{4}=\tfrac{\pi^2}{16}<\infty.$$

**Application of the Dominated Convergence Theorem.** The sequence $\{f_n\}$ satisfies:
1. $f_n\to 0$ pointwise on $[0,\pi/4]$,
2. $|f_n(x)|\le g(x)$ for all $n\ge1$ and all $x\in[0,\pi/4]$, with $g\in L^1([0,\pi/4])$.

By the Dominated Convergence Theorem,
$$L=\lim_{n\to\infty}\int_{[0,\pi/4]} f_n\,d\lambda = \int_{[0,\pi/4]}\lim_{n\to\infty}f_n\,d\lambda = \int_{[0,\pi/4]} 0\,d\lambda = 0.$$

**Conclusion.** The critical fact is that the entire domain $[0,\pi/4]$ lies strictly inside $(-1,1)$, so $x^n\to 0$ uniformly-boundedly fast (exponentially, bounded by $(\pi/4)^n$), forcing $\log(1+x^n)\to 0$ and making the oscillatory factor $\sin(nx)$ irrelevant.

$$\boxed{\displaystyle\lim_{n\to\infty}\int_{[0,\pi/4]}\sin(nx)\log(1+x^n)\,d\lambda(x)=0}$$

